<page xmlns="http://projectmallard.org/1.0/"
      type="topic" id="elements">
  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#elements"/>
    <title type="sort">0</title>
    <link type="seealso" xref="canvas"/>
    <link type="seealso" xref="properties-panel"/>
    <desc>Why an element's box is the size it is, why some boxes snap back
    after a drag, and the ZPL each element becomes.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>How elements work</title>

  <p>Everything on a label is an element: a line of text, a barcode, a box, a
  picture. Each one becomes a few lines of ZPL, the language Zebra printers
  read, and the canvas draws it the way the printer will print it. Most of
  what can look odd about the canvas, such as text that will not stretch or
  a barcode that springs back after a drag, comes from that.</p>

  <section id="dots">
    <title>Everything is measured in dots</title>
    <p>A thermal printer prints rows of tiny dots, and every position and size
    in LinuxZPL is a whole number of them. How big a dot is depends on the
    printer's resolution:</p>
    <table frame="all" rules="rows">
      <thead>
        <tr><td><p>Resolution</p></td><td><p>Dots per millimetre</p></td>
        <td><p>Dots per inch</p></td><td><p>One dot</p></td></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><p>203 dpi</p></td><td><p>8</p></td><td><p>203</p></td><td><p>0.125 mm</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>300 dpi</p></td><td><p>12</p></td><td><p>300</p></td><td><p>0.083 mm</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>600 dpi</p></td><td><p>24</p></td><td><p>600</p></td><td><p>0.042 mm</p></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p><gui>X</gui> and <gui>Y</gui> in the Properties panel are the top left
    corner of the element's box, counted in dots from the label's top left
    corner. The same design is a different size on paper at another
    resolution: see <link xref="resolution"/>.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="box">
    <title>The box is what prints</title>
    <p>Each element's box is measured the way the printer will print it:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>Text is measured from its font, so the box is as wide as the
      printed words.</p></item>
      <item><p>A barcode is measured from its value once it has been
      encoded, so the box is as wide as the printed bars.</p></item>
      <item><p>A circle's box is its diameter, and a frame's box is its
      outside edge.</p></item>
    </list>
    <p>The outlines, the light tint behind text and frames, and the handles
    are there only to show you the element. None of them prints. The box does
    not include the blank margin a barcode needs either side for a scanner:
    leave that clear yourself (see <link xref="barcode#size"/>).</p>
  </section>

  <section id="turned">
    <title>Turned elements</title>
    <p>An element turned 90° or 270° still has an upright box: its width and
    height simply swap. Selecting, dragging, the handles and
    <gui>Align</gui> all work on that box, the same at every turn.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="handles">
    <title>What the handles change</title>
    <p>Dragging a handle does not always just stretch the box. For many
    elements it asks for a new value of one of the element's own settings,
    and the box then becomes whatever that setting prints:</p>
    <table frame="all" rules="rows">
      <thead>
        <tr><td><p>Element</p></td><td><p>A handle changes</p></td></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><p>Frame, ellipse, diagonal line, image</p></td>
        <td><p>The box itself, exactly as dragged.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Text</p></td>
        <td><p>The font height (top and bottom handles) and the font width
        (side handles).</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Wrapped text</p></td>
        <td><p>The font, as for text, and the block grows or shrinks with it:
        the side handles also widen or narrow the wrap width, so the lines
        break where they did. To re-flow the text, set the wrap width, lines
        or height in the Properties panel.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Barcode</p></td>
        <td><p>The module width (sides) and the bar height (top and bottom).
        A two-dimensional code has one size, the largest that fits the box
        you dragged.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Circle</p></td>
        <td><p>Its one diameter.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Graphic symbol</p></td>
        <td><p>Its size, in steps of 24 dots.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Stored graphic</p></td>
        <td><p>Its magnification, once LinuxZPL has seen the graphic; until
        then, the stand-in box.</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>MaxiCode, TLC39, a PDF417 split over several symbols, and
        a stored graphic printed with <gui>Image Move</gui></p></td>
        <td><p>Nothing: they have no handles, because the printer decides
        their size.</p></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </section>

  <section id="snap">
    <title>Why some boxes snap back</title>
    <p>The printer can only print whole dots, whole barcode modules and whole
    steps of a built-in font. So when a drag asks for a size between two that
    can print, the box comes back to the nearest one that can, the moment you
    move the pointer. What you see after the drag is what will print, not the
    rectangle you dragged. The box grows or shrinks from the edge you did not
    drag, so the element stays where you put it.</p>
    <p>For the same reason, text and barcodes have no <gui>Width</gui> row in
    the Properties panel. Their width follows from the text, the font and the
    module width, so change one of those instead.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="limits">
    <title>Limits</title>
    <list>
      <item><p>A handle never makes a side smaller than 20 dots, so an
      element can't be squeezed out of reach of the pointer. Type a smaller
      size in the Properties panel instead. A side that is already smaller,
      such as a 3-dot rule's thickness, keeps its size when you drag a handle
      on the other side.</p></item>
      <item><p>Nothing can be dragged off the label. An element pushed
      against an edge stays against it.</p></item>
      <item><p>Typed sizes go as far as ZPL allows, mostly 32000 dots, and
      are not held to the label's size.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="ink">
    <title>Black, white and Reverse</title>
    <p>A thermal printer can only add black; it cannot erase. So:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>Elements that overlap all print. An image laid over text does
      not hide the text: the text prints through the image's white parts,
      and the canvas shows it that way.</p></item>
      <item><p>A shape whose <gui>Colour</gui> is <gui>White</gui> prints
      nothing on bare labels. It shows only where it lies over something
      black.</p></item>
      <item><p><gui>Reverse</gui> prints an element white wherever something
      is already printed beneath it, and black elsewhere. "Beneath" means
      earlier in the stacking order, which is also what decides which
      element a click picks (see <link xref="canvas#order"/>).</p></item>
      <item><p>To keep an element in the design but off the printed label,
      clear <gui>Print This Element</gui>.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="groups">
    <title>Groups</title>
    <p>Dragging a selected group's handles scales every element in it, each
    by its own rule: text changes its font size, a barcode its module width
    and bar height, a circle stays round. The group therefore ends up close
    to, but not always exactly, the size you dragged.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="from-elsewhere">
    <title>Labels made by other programs</title>
    <p>LinuxZPL opens ZPL written by other programs and keeps how they
    placed each field, so saving the label does not move anything:</p>
    <list>
      <item><p>A field placed by its text's baseline (<code>^FT</code>) rather
      than its top left corner (<code>^FO</code>) is saved the same way.
      <gui>X</gui> and <gui>Y</gui> still show the top left of its
      box.</p></item>
      <item><p>A field pinned by its right edge keeps that edge still: longer
      text grows to the left.</p></item>
      <item><p>A field with no position of its own, which the printer puts
      where the previous field ended, keeps following that field until you
      drag it on its own.</p></item>
      <item><p>A field the file places partly off the label is left there,
      since moving it would change what prints.</p></item>
      <item><p>The author's comments (<code>^FX</code>) stay with their
      fields.</p></item>
      <item><p>Commands LinuxZPL does not understand are listed when the
      file opens. They are not drawn, and saving does not keep
      them.</p></item>
    </list>
  </section>

  <section id="zpl">
    <title>The ZPL each element writes</title>
    <p>Useful when reading a label file, or when typing commands in the
    <link xref="printer-status#console">printer console</link>. Every field
    starts at <code>^FO</code> (or <code>^FT</code>) and ends at
    <code>^FS</code>, and <gui>Reverse</gui> adds <code>^FR</code>.</p>
    <table frame="all" rules="rows">
      <thead>
        <tr><td><p>Element</p></td><td><p>ZPL</p></td></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><p>Text</p></td><td><p><code>^A</code> (a built-in font) or
        <code>^A@</code> (a TrueType font by name), then <code>^FD</code> and
        the text; <code>^FB</code> or <code>^TB</code> when it wraps, and
        <code>^FP</code> for its direction and character gap</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Date or time</p></td><td><p><code>^FC</code>, with the
        format in <code>^FD</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Serial number</p></td><td><p><code>^SN</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Numbered field</p></td><td><p><code>^FN</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Frame</p></td><td><p><code>^GB</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Circle</p></td><td><p><code>^GC</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Ellipse</p></td><td><p><code>^GE</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Diagonal line</p></td><td><p><code>^GD</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Graphic symbol</p></td><td><p><code>^GS</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Barcode</p></td><td><p><code>^BY</code> for the module
        width, then the barcode's own command, such as <code>^BC</code> for
        Code 128 or <code>^BQ</code> for a QR Code, and the value in
        <code>^FD</code></p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Image</p></td><td><p><code>^GF</code>, the picture's dots
        written out in full</p></td></tr>
        <tr><td><p>Stored graphic</p></td><td><p><code>^XG</code> (Recall
        Graphic) or <code>^IM</code> (Image Move), naming the graphic on the
        printer</p></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>The label itself starts with <code>^XA</code>, gives its size with
    <code>^PW</code> and <code>^LL</code>, and ends with <code>^XZ</code>.
    What only the designer needs, such as the resolution and groups, goes in
    <code>^FX</code> comments, which printers ignore.</p>
  </section>
</page>
